# SkyDome Polar Cloud Layout

`SkyDome Polar Cloud Layout` starts the cloud volume pipeline from an existing
polar SkyDome UV texture. It does not generate concept art first. The input
texture is treated as the approved sky composition.

## Scope

The first pass started as a no-C++, editor-only project tool under:

```text
MCPGameProject/Plugins/SkyDomeTools
```

It uses the external pinned Python 3.11 `uv --directory .\Python ...`
environment for image processing. Unreal Python is only used for editor menu
glue, texture/card asset creation, actor placement, and screenshot capture.

Do not add runtime modules, runtime components, gameplay dependencies, or
packaging dependencies for this tool. C++/API work is allowed only for narrow
editor automation gaps after the Python route is insufficient. The current VDB
preview uses one such editor-only helper in the `SkyDomeTools` module to apply
the imported Sparse Volume Texture frame transform to a Heterogeneous Volume
component, because Unreal Python exposes `frame_transform` as read-only.

## Pipeline

```text
Polar UV texture input
-> sky disc mask detection
-> cloud mask extraction
-> connected cloud module generation
-> module crop/mask/card texture output
-> Unreal card actor placement on a polar validation board
-> viewport screenshot validation
-> front-view redraw per validated module
-> Keilan imagegen front-concept planning
-> Hunyuan3D batch mesh generation, texture disabled
-> Blender VDB conversion
-> Unreal Sparse Volume Texture import
-> card actor replacement with VDB actor
-> UDS static sky capture/bake
```

## Review Gate

Hunyuan generation is blocked until the Unreal card layout screenshot is
validated. The manifest records this as:

```text
card_layout_validated_before_hunyuan
```

The goal of the card stage is not final cloud beauty. It verifies that the
original polar UV composition survives modularization. By default, card actors
are placed on a dark Y/Z validation board (`--placement-space polar_validation`)
so the screenshot can be reviewed against the original polar UV image. The
manifest still stores dome-space positions for later Hunyuan/VDB replacement.

## Commands

Extract cloud modules from a polar UV texture:

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python ..\MCPGameProject\Plugins\SkyDomeTools\Scripts\skydome_polar_cloud_layout.py `
  --input C:\Path\To\PolarClouds.png `
  --layout-name MyPolarClouds `
  --extract `
  --json
```

Place extracted modules as Unreal card actors:

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python ..\MCPGameProject\Plugins\SkyDomeTools\Scripts\skydome_polar_cloud_layout.py `
  --manifest .\MCPGameProject\Saved\SkyDomeTools\PolarCloudLayout\MyPolarClouds\polar_layout_manifest.json `
  --place-cards `
  --placement-space polar_validation `
  --json
```

For dome-space preview placement, pass `--placement-space dome`. Do not use the
dome preview as the Hunyuan gate; the polar validation screenshot is the gate.

Capture the active editor viewport for validation:

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python ..\MCPGameProject\Plugins\SkyDomeTools\Scripts\skydome_polar_cloud_layout.py `
  --manifest .\MCPGameProject\Saved\SkyDomeTools\PolarCloudLayout\MyPolarClouds\polar_layout_manifest.json `
  --capture-screenshot `
  --json
```

Run the full first-pass layout automation:

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python ..\MCPGameProject\Plugins\SkyDomeTools\Scripts\skydome_polar_cloud_layout.py `
  --input C:\Path\To\PolarClouds.png `
  --layout-name MyPolarClouds `
  --auto `
  --json
```

The editor menu entry is:

```text
SkyDome Tools > Polar Cloud Layout
```

## Hunyuan Batch

Before starting or reusing the local Hunyuan API server, inspect the mesh-only
worker path. SkyDome cloud jobs run Hunyuan with `texture=false`; the local
`model_worker.py` must return the initial mesh before texture generation, load
the paint pipeline lazily, and avoid deleting cache directories during startup.

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python ..\MCPGameProject\Plugins\SkyDomeTools\Scripts\skydome_hunyuan_environment.py `
  --hunyuan-root D:\Git\Hunyuan3D-2.1 `
  --json
```

If the check reports `mesh_only_api_ready: false`, patch the worker and restart
the Hunyuan API server:

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python ..\MCPGameProject\Plugins\SkyDomeTools\Scripts\skydome_hunyuan_environment.py `
  --hunyuan-root D:\Git\Hunyuan3D-2.1 `
  --patch-texture-false `
  --json
```

The Hunyuan runner performs this inspection before direct API generation. If a
local `model_worker.py` is found and is not mesh-only ready, the job fails before
calling `/generate`. The standalone Hunyuan UI and the Polar Cloud Layout UI
also expose `Check Env`, `Patch Env`, and `Start API` buttons. The Polar UI's
`Hunyuan Root` field is passed through to the batch runner, so use the same root
that will run the local API server.

To inspect the API server command without loading the model:

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python ..\MCPGameProject\Plugins\SkyDomeTools\Scripts\skydome_hunyuan_api_server.py `
  --hunyuan-root D:\Git\Hunyuan3D-2.1 `
  --api-url http://127.0.0.1:8081 `
  --dry-run `
  --json
```

To start the local API server from the same path used by the UI:

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python ..\MCPGameProject\Plugins\SkyDomeTools\Scripts\skydome_hunyuan_api_server.py `
  --hunyuan-root D:\Git\Hunyuan3D-2.1 `
  --api-url http://127.0.0.1:8081 `
  --start `
  --json
```

The server launcher uses `D:\Git\Hunyuan3D-2.1\.venv\Scripts\python.exe`,
`api_server.py`, and an isolated cache folder such as `api_cache_8081`. It writes
stdout/stderr logs under that cache folder and records the launched PID. Model
loading can take several minutes, so a successful launch may report `starting`
before `/health` is ready.

After the polar validation screenshot passes, run a dry-run queue first:

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python ..\MCPGameProject\Plugins\SkyDomeTools\Scripts\skydome_polar_hunyuan_batch.py `
  --manifest .\MCPGameProject\Saved\SkyDomeTools\PolarCloudLayout\MyPolarClouds\polar_layout_manifest.json `
  --dry-run `
  --json
```

Then run the batch against the local Hunyuan API:

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python ..\MCPGameProject\Plugins\SkyDomeTools\Scripts\skydome_polar_hunyuan_batch.py `
  --manifest .\MCPGameProject\Saved\SkyDomeTools\PolarCloudLayout\MyPolarClouds\polar_layout_manifest.json `
  --api-url http://127.0.0.1:8081 `
  --ensure-api-server `
  --json
```

`--ensure-api-server` starts the local Hunyuan API server when `/health` is not
ready. If the process launches but model loading is still in progress, the batch
stops before calling `/generate` and reports `api_server_starting`; rerun the
same batch after health becomes ready. Use `--api-start-wait-seconds` to wait
longer before deciding that the model is still loading.

To continue directly into VDB generation, keep texture generation off and pass
the Blender volume options through the batch runner:

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python ..\MCPGameProject\Plugins\SkyDomeTools\Scripts\skydome_polar_hunyuan_batch.py `
  --manifest .\MCPGameProject\Saved\SkyDomeTools\PolarCloudLayout\MyPolarClouds\polar_layout_manifest.json `
  --api-url http://127.0.0.1:8081 `
  --run-blender `
  --import-vdb-to-unreal `
  --vdb-placement-space polar_validation `
  --vdb-depth-fraction 0.25 `
  --blender-voxel-amount 192 `
  --blender-min-thickness-fraction 0.42 `
  --blender-vdb-edge-softness-voxels 10 `
  --blender-vdb-density-scale 0.65 `
  --blender-vdb-density-threshold 0.004 `
  --blender-vdb-noise-strength 0.18 `
  --blender-vdb-noise-frequency 0.11 `
  --blender-vdb-density-profile solid `
  --unreal-preview-density-scale 0.003 `
  --json
```

The batch runner uses `front_concept_path` first. If that image is not present,
it uses the generated `hunyuan_reference_path`, which is a normalized, enlarged
module card intended only as a temporary Hunyuan input. If that is also missing,
it can fall back to `front_redraw_guide_path`. Pass `--no-guide-input` when
strict front-redraw art is required. Texture generation is forced off for this
pipeline. When `remove_background` is enabled, the Hunyuan runner also prepares a
job-local `hunyuan_reference_rgba.png` with alpha cut from the concept image's
corner background and records it as `hunyuan_reference_image`; this avoids relying
on the local Hunyuan API server's background-removal flag. The runner writes
generated mesh paths back to `hunyuan_mesh_path` in the polar manifest.

The Polar Cloud Layout UI exposes `Hunyuan Dry Run` and `Run Hunyuan Batch`
buttons after a manifest exists. It also exposes `Hunyuan Root`, `Check Env`,
`Patch Env`, and `Start API` before a manifest exists, so the local Hunyuan
worker and API server can be prepared before generation starts. The UI's
`Auto Start API` toggle passes `ensure_api_server=True` to the batch runner for
non-dry-run Hunyuan batches.

## Keilan Front Concepts

Keilan owns the temporary imagegen front-concept pass before Hunyuan. The first
tooling step is a dry run that plans cloud-by-cloud imagegen prompts and output
paths without making any imagegen request:

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python ..\MCPGameProject\Plugins\SkyDomeTools\Scripts\skydome_polar_keilan_concept.py `
  --manifest .\MCPGameProject\Saved\SkyDomeTools\PolarCloudLayout\MyPolarClouds\polar_layout_manifest.json `
  --dry-run `
  --json
```

The dry run writes `keilan_concept_prompt_manifest.json` beside the polar
manifest and records a `keilan_concept_batch` summary in the manifest. Planned
concept outputs use `modules/Cloud_###_keilan_concept.png`. This pass does not
write `front_concept_path`; that field is only assigned after an actual imagegen
artifact exists and is accepted. The audit report exposes
`keilan_concept_needed_cloud_ids` for modules that still use the generated
`hunyuan_reference_path` fallback instead of a Keilan or hand-authored
`front_concept_path`.

After Codex built-in image generation creates a concept PNG, accept it into the
polar manifest:

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python ..\MCPGameProject\Plugins\SkyDomeTools\Scripts\skydome_polar_keilan_concept.py `
  --manifest .\MCPGameProject\Saved\SkyDomeTools\PolarCloudLayout\MyPolarClouds\polar_layout_manifest.json `
  --cloud-id Cloud_001 `
  --generated-image Cloud_001=C:\Path\To\Cloud_001_keilan_concept.png `
  --accept-generated `
  --json
```

The accept pass copies the supplied image into the planned
`modules/Cloud_###_keilan_concept.png` path when needed, validates that it is a
usable square image, writes `keilan_concept_accept_manifest.json`, and records
`front_concept_path`, `front_concept_source`, and `front_concept_validation` on
the matching cloud module. It does not call `OPENAI_API_KEY` or any API-key
image route. To ingest a folder of already copied Codex generated images, use
`--copy-generated-from <folder>`; filenames must contain the matching cloud id.
If a module already has a Hunyuan mesh when the concept is accepted, the tool
marks `hunyuan_rerun_required` so the audit and resume paths regenerate that
module from the accepted `front_concept_path` instead of silently keeping the
older generated-reference mesh. If a previous UDS static capture exists, the
tool also marks it stale because the baked sky no longer reflects the current
module source art.

## Blender VDB And Unreal Preview

The Blender VDB runner converts the Hunyuan mesh through the local Blender
executable and writes a fog `density` grid. The VDB writer prunes low-density
voxels through `--vdb-density-threshold` before saving the OpenVDB file. The
default is `0.02`; higher values such as `0.15` or `0.30` are useful when a
test mesh leaves a visible low-density volume box in Unreal.

The OpenVDB fallback writer keeps `exterior_band_voxels` at least one voxel
larger than `edge_softness_voxels`. Without that guard, the level-set background
falls inside the soft shell and the whole active bounding box can become a faint
fog cube.

The Hunyuan runner, polar batch runner, resume runner, and Blender UI now share
the same sky-soft VDB defaults: `--blender-voxel-amount 192`,
`--blender-min-thickness-fraction 0.42`,
`--blender-vdb-edge-softness-voxels 10`, `--blender-vdb-density-scale 0.65`,
`--blender-vdb-density-threshold 0.004`, `--blender-vdb-noise-strength 0.18`,
`--blender-vdb-noise-frequency 0.11`, and `--blender-vdb-density-profile solid`.
The paired Unreal preview default is `--unreal-preview-density-scale 0.003`.
This replaces the earlier 96-voxel shell profile, which validated the technical
pipeline but enlarged into visible voxel/plane artifacts during sky capture.
Increase voxel amount only after a module still shows blocky edges; lower
preview density before lowering written VDB density when a capture is simply too
white.
The Blender VDB quality metrics now also flag `normalized_mesh_elongated_slab`
for long, thin Hunyuan results and `density_high_contrast_shell` for dense shell
exports that tend to bake as hard white plates. Treat those as source mesh or
front-concept problems first; do not spend more static-bake time on those modules
until the Hunyuan mesh is regenerated from a better cloud concept.

When `--import-vdb-to-unreal` is enabled from the polar batch runner, preview
actors use each module's recorded card location, rotation, width, and height
instead of the generic VDB preview scale. Use `--vdb-placement-space
polar_validation` for the first validation pass, because it places the generated
volume over the same board used for card layout approval. Use
`--vdb-placement-space dome` only after module-scale validation passes.

The Unreal VDB importer creates a Sparse Volume Texture and a Heterogeneous
Volume preview actor. The preview material is a `MaterialInstanceConstant`
parented to Unreal's `/Engine/EngineMaterials/SparseVolumeMaterial`; the tool
sets the SVT parameter plus conservative cloud defaults such as low `Density
Scale` (currently `0.003` for large sky-module previews), white `Albedo Color`,
`Density (Attributes A)` enabled, `Temperature (Attributes B)` disabled, and
disabled blackbody contribution. The preview
actor then applies the SVT volume resolution and, when the editor C++ helper is
loaded, the SVT frame transform to the Heterogeneous Volume component. Current
Ieta validation accepts this as the correct technical path; if the helper is
unavailable, Unreal's component `frame_transform` can remain identity during
editor automation and a visible volume bounds box may appear.

The importer exposes preview material intensity controls:

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python ..\MCPGameProject\Plugins\SkyDomeTools\Scripts\skydome_vdb_importer.py `
  --spawn-preview `
  --preview-svt /Game/SkyDomeTools/VDB/GeneratedClouds/MyLayout/SVT_MyCloud_VDB `
  --preview-density-scale 0.02 `
  --json
```

The polar Hunyuan batch and resume commands pass the same control through as
`--unreal-preview-density-scale`. Use this for viewport QA and material tuning;
do not confuse it with Blender's `--blender-vdb-density-scale`, which changes
the written OpenVDB data.

If reducing `Density Scale` does not change a hard white slab in the viewport,
the capture is probably dominated by source mesh silhouette or unclamped SVT
density. Use the viewport tools' `--clamp-svt-density`,
`--density-clamp-min`, and `--density-clamp-max` options as capture-scope
diagnostics before accepting a static sky bake. Current validation showed that
the clamp parameters apply and restore correctly, but they do not materially fix
the hard white slab artifacts in the current Hunyuan meshes; problem modules
still require source concept or Hunyuan mesh regeneration.

When validating screenshots, enable Level Editor Game View so editor sprites and
selection proxies do not masquerade as volume artifacts. Avoid treating high
prune thresholds such as `0.30` as a final cloud look: they are useful for
diagnosing bounds artifacts, but can remove low-density edge voxels and make the
cloud read as a hard voxelized mesh in Unreal. Prefer the default low threshold
or a softer Blender VDB pass for beauty validation.

### VDB Viewport QA

After VDB preview actors exist, use the viewport QA helper to capture multiple
active-editor views with UDS dynamic clouds disabled and card actors hidden:

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python ..\MCPGameProject\Plugins\SkyDomeTools\Scripts\skydome_vdb_viewport_qa.py `
  --manifest .\MCPGameProject\Saved\SkyDomeTools\PolarCloudLayout\MyPolarClouds\polar_layout_manifest.json `
  --cloud-id Cloud_001 `
  --cloud-id Cloud_002 `
  --cloud-id Cloud_003 `
  --density-scale 0.02 `
  --radius-multiplier 1.0 `
  --scale-multiplier 0.75 `
  --depth-fraction 0.5 `
  --distance 35000 `
  --view-fov 70 `
  --clean-game-view `
  --json
```

The helper writes per-view PNGs, `vdb_viewport_qa_contact_sheet.png`, and
`vdb_viewport_qa_result.json` under `Saved/SkyDomeTools/VisualQA`. By default it
restores actor transforms, CVar values, temporary card visibility, and material
density after capture. When the updated UnrealMCP editor bridge is loaded, it
also sends temporary `view_location`, `view_rotation`, and `view_fov` values
directly to `capture_viewport_bookmark_screenshot` and restores the previous
viewport camera after every PNG. Current validation showed that
`r.VolumetricCloud 0` removes UDS dynamic cloud contamination, while remaining
hard planes are VDB preview bounds/volume-quality artifacts rather than polar
card actors.

### Viewport Static Bake

Use the viewport static bake helper after VDB preview placement passes viewport
QA. This path exists because Unreal's `SceneCaptureCube` path does not reliably
capture Heterogeneous Volume VDB previews in this project, while the active
Level Editor viewport does. The helper captures six clean viewport directions
with temporary camera overrides, center-crops each face to a square, stitches the
faces to a lat-long PNG, and writes a cube-face contact sheet plus result JSON:

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python ..\MCPGameProject\Plugins\SkyDomeTools\Scripts\skydome_viewport_static_bake.py `
  --manifest .\MCPGameProject\Saved\SkyDomeTools\PolarCloudLayout\MyPolarClouds\polar_layout_manifest.json `
  --density-scale 0.003 `
  --radius-multiplier 1.0 `
  --scale-multiplier 2.0 `
  --depth-fraction 0.75 `
  --camera-origin 0,0,0 `
  --equirect-width 4096 `
  --equirect-height 2048 `
  --hide-non-sky-actors `
  --hide-post-process `
  --albedo-scale 0.45 `
  --albedo-color 1.0,0.92,0.78,1.0 `
  --clamp-svt-density `
  --density-clamp-min 0.0 `
  --density-clamp-max 0.15 `
  --import-texture `
  --texture-destination-path /Game/SkyDomeTools/UDS/Textures `
  --bake-asset-name SkyDome_StaticCloudBake `
  --json
```

Outputs are written under `Saved/SkyDomeTools/ViewportStaticBake` by default:
`cube_px.png` through `cube_nz.png`, `viewport_static_bake_cube_faces.png`,
`viewport_static_bake_equirect.png`, and `viewport_static_bake_result.json`.
The helper disables `r.VolumetricCloud`, enables `r.HeterogeneousVolumes`, hides
the polar card actor prefixes, applies manifest VDB transforms, sets preview
material density, and restores those temporary state changes after capture.
Pass `--hide-non-sky-actors` when baking inside a populated UDS demo or preview
map; it temporarily keeps only `Ultra_Dynamic_Sky`, `PostProcessVolume`, and
`HeterogeneousVolume` actors plus `SkyDomeTools_HunyuanVDBPreview_` labels, then
restores the previous hidden states after capture.
Pass `--hide-post-process` when the demo-map post-process volume is making the
VDB preview too bloomy or too exposure-dependent. The bake then keeps UDS sky and
Heterogeneous Volume actors but hides `PostProcessVolume` for the capture scope.
The `--albedo-scale`, `--blackbody-scale`, and `--albedo-color` options are also
temporary capture-scope material overrides; they are restored after the viewport
screenshots finish.
The `--clamp-svt-density`, `--density-clamp-min`, and
`--density-clamp-max` options are restored the same way and are intended for
preview/material tuning, not for hiding source-quality failures.
When `--import-texture` is passed, it imports the stitched equirect PNG as a
Texture2D using the existing SkyDomeTools PNG import route. Interchange import
is disabled by default during that import, matching the project rule for PNG
imports; pass `--keep-interchange-import` only for explicit diagnosis.

The script probes the active viewport size and computes a wider horizontal
viewport FOV before cropping so the square center crop approximates a 90-degree
cube face. Pass `--source-view-fov` only when this automatic compensation needs
manual override for a known viewport aspect.

## Pipeline Audit

After any card, Hunyuan, Blender VDB, or Unreal preview pass, audit the manifest
before deciding the next run. The audit does not mutate the manifest or generate
assets; it reads the recorded module paths and writes a Markdown report when
requested.

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python ..\MCPGameProject\Plugins\SkyDomeTools\Scripts\skydome_polar_pipeline_audit.py `
  --manifest .\MCPGameProject\Saved\SkyDomeTools\PolarCloudLayout\MyPolarClouds\polar_layout_manifest.json `
  --write-report `
  --json
```

Use `--require-front-concept` when the Hunyuan stage must wait for hand-painted
or generated front-view concept images instead of using the temporary
`hunyuan_reference_path` fallback. The report separates these queues:

- `keilan_concept_needed_cloud_ids`
- `front_concept_needed_cloud_ids`
- `hunyuan_cloud_ids`
- `blender_vdb_cloud_ids`
- `unreal_vdb_preview_cloud_ids`
- `uds_ready_cloud_ids`
- `pipeline_complete_cloud_ids`

`hunyuan_cloud_ids` also includes modules marked with
`hunyuan_rerun_required`, even when older mesh, VDB, or preview paths still
exist in the manifest.

The Polar Cloud Layout UI exposes an `Audit` button after a manifest exists,
an `Open Audit` button after the full Markdown report is written, and an
`Open Review` button after the standalone review checklist is written. The
Audit status message also prints the next action, UDS readiness, completion
flag, and a short warning-count summary so repeated quality issues are visible
without opening the report. When review queues are present, the UI also prints
queue counts for front-concept fallback and VDB quality review targets, followed
by the generated report, checklist, and compact review JSON paths.

The Markdown report includes `Final Outputs` for the UDS capture asset, baked
texture asset, and exported PNG paths when they are recorded in the manifest.
It also includes `Warning Counts`, which groups repeated module warnings such as
temporary front-reference fallbacks or VDB density issues for final review.
Completed pipelines can still have quality review work. The report keeps that
separate from the executable `requeue` section under `Review Queues`:

- `front_concept_fallback_cloud_ids` lists modules that used generated
  `hunyuan_reference_path` images instead of final `front_concept_path` art.
- `vdb_quality_warning_cloud_ids` lists modules with Blender VDB quality
  warnings such as high density fill, saturated density, or thin normalized
  meshes.
- `all_review_cloud_ids` merges the review targets without changing
  `pipeline_complete` or automatic resume behavior.

The same report also writes `Review Checklist` items. Each item records the
cloud id, review reason, matched warning codes, reference image source, and
recorded front-concept/VDB paths so the next pass can open the report and work
module-by-module without re-reading the JSON manifest. Checklist entries also
include conservative `next:` hints, for example assigning a final
`front_concept_path`, increasing `--blender-min-thickness-fraction` for thin
meshes, or tuning VDB density parameters after preview inspection.
When `--write-report` or the UI Audit button runs, the tool writes the full
report to `polar_pipeline_audit.md` and the standalone checklist to
`polar_review_checklist.md` beside the manifest. It also writes compact review
data to `polar_review_items.json` so automation can read just the review queues,
items, and warning counts without parsing the full audit output.
Use `--review-checklist-path` to write that checklist to a custom path; this can
be used with `--write-report` or by itself when automation only needs the
review checklist artifact. Use `--review-json-path` the same way for the compact
review JSON artifact.

## Pipeline Resume

Use the resume command after an audit when part of the pipeline already
completed. This command consumes the audit queues and avoids rerunning completed
work. In particular, a module with `hunyuan_mesh_path` but no `vdb_path` can run
Blender VDB generation without calling Hunyuan again.

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python ..\MCPGameProject\Plugins\SkyDomeTools\Scripts\skydome_polar_pipeline_resume.py `
  --manifest .\MCPGameProject\Saved\SkyDomeTools\PolarCloudLayout\MyPolarClouds\polar_layout_manifest.json `
  --dry-run `
  --json
```

Resume existing meshes into VDBs and import previews only when Unreal is
reachable:

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python ..\MCPGameProject\Plugins\SkyDomeTools\Scripts\skydome_polar_pipeline_resume.py `
  --manifest .\MCPGameProject\Saved\SkyDomeTools\PolarCloudLayout\MyPolarClouds\polar_layout_manifest.json `
  --run-blender-vdb `
  --import-vdb-preview `
  --json
```

To rerun only modules that are in the VDB quality review queue, without calling
Hunyuan again, use:

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python ..\MCPGameProject\Plugins\SkyDomeTools\Scripts\skydome_polar_pipeline_resume.py `
  --manifest .\MCPGameProject\Saved\SkyDomeTools\PolarCloudLayout\MyPolarClouds\polar_layout_manifest.json `
  --rerun-vdb-quality `
  --import-vdb-preview `
  --json
```

This path is intended for warnings such as `density_fill_ratio_high`. It
regenerates the VDB from the existing `hunyuan_mesh_path`, clears stale preview
references, imports the new Sparse Volume Texture preview, and marks an older
UDS static capture stale when needed.

If Unreal MCP is unavailable, preview import is skipped and recorded in the JSON
result rather than failing the Blender VDB resume. Hunyuan batch execution is
not implicit; pass `--run-hunyuan --no-skip-unavailable` only after the Hunyuan
API or a `--command-template` route is ready. To let resume start or verify the
local Hunyuan API before delegating to the batch runner, add
`--ensure-api-server`:

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python ..\MCPGameProject\Plugins\SkyDomeTools\Scripts\skydome_polar_pipeline_resume.py `
  --manifest .\MCPGameProject\Saved\SkyDomeTools\PolarCloudLayout\MyPolarClouds\polar_layout_manifest.json `
  --auto `
  --no-skip-unavailable `
  --ensure-api-server `
  --json
```

The Polar Cloud Layout UI exposes `Resume Dry Run`, `Resume VDB`, and
`Resume Auto` buttons for this path. `Resume Auto` runs Hunyuan, Blender VDB, and
Unreal preview queues, and uses the `Auto Start API` toggle for Hunyuan API
preflight.

For the full automatic path into the UDS static sky bake, add
`--capture-uds-static-sky`. The capture stage is still gated: it only runs after
the final audit reports every module as `ready_for_uds_capture`. If Hunyuan, VDB,
or Unreal preview work remains, the resume command records a skipped UDS capture
instead of baking a partial sky.

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python ..\MCPGameProject\Plugins\SkyDomeTools\Scripts\skydome_polar_pipeline_resume.py `
  --manifest .\MCPGameProject\Saved\SkyDomeTools\PolarCloudLayout\MyPolarClouds\polar_layout_manifest.json `
  --auto `
  --no-skip-unavailable `
  --ensure-api-server `
  --capture-uds-static-sky `
  --json
```

When launched from the Polar Cloud Layout UI, the `Capture UDS Static Sky`
toggle controls this same final capture stage for `Resume Auto`. The generated
capture and bake asset names default to the layout name, for example
`MyPolarClouds_StaticSkyCube` and `T_MyPolarClouds_StaticCloudBake`. After a
successful capture, the Polar UI enables `Open UDS`, which syncs the Content
Browser to the generated UDS texture folder.

The polar UDS capture path treats UDS dynamic volumetric clouds as contamination:
it scopes `r.VolumetricCloud 0` and `r.HeterogeneousVolumes 1` around the capture,
then restores the previous values. This keeps the bake focused on generated VDB
volumes while still allowing UDS sky, atmosphere, and lighting to contribute.

After a successful UDS bake, the manifest records `uds_static_capture.success`.
The audit then reports `next_action: pipeline_complete` and clears the UDS ready
queue. Re-running resume with `--capture-uds-static-sky` on a completed manifest
does not recapture; it reports the capture stage as already complete. If a later
Keilan concept, Hunyuan mesh, Blender VDB, or Unreal preview pass updates cloud
content, the tools mark `uds_static_capture_stale`; audit then returns
`ready_for_uds_capture` until the static sky is captured again.

## Outputs

The tool writes a folder under:

```text
MCPGameProject/Saved/SkyDomeTools/PolarCloudLayout/<LayoutName>
```

Important outputs:

- `polar_layout_manifest.json`
- `polar_cloud_modules_overlay.png`
- `polar_cloud_mask.png`
- `modules/Cloud_###_polar_crop.png`
- `modules/Cloud_###_mask.png`
- `modules/Cloud_###_card.png`
- `modules/Cloud_###_front_redraw_guide.png`
- `modules/Cloud_###_hunyuan_reference.png`
- `keilan_concept_prompt_manifest.json` after a Keilan dry run
- `keilan_concept_accept_manifest.json` after accepting Keilan concept PNGs
- `polar_cloud_cards_viewport.png` after screenshot capture
- `polar_pipeline_audit.md` after a pipeline audit report
- `polar_review_checklist.md` after a pipeline audit report
- `polar_review_items.json` after a pipeline audit report
- `hunyuan_batch.batch_manifest` after a Hunyuan dry run or batch run

The generated Unreal assets are editor validation assets under:

```text
/Game/SkyDomeTools/PolarCloudLayout/<LayoutName>
```

The Unreal validation placement also creates a generated dark backdrop material
and actor behind the cards so UDS sky clouds do not hide card silhouettes in the
viewport screenshot.

Do not commit generated `.uasset` files unless the user explicitly asks for
sample assets.

Generated editor validation assets are ignored under these paths:

- `MCPGameProject/Content/SkyDomeTools/UDS/`
- `MCPGameProject/Content/SkyDomeTools/VDB/GeneratedClouds/`
- `MCPGameProject/Content/SkyDomeTools/VDB/GeneratedTests/`
- transient generated materials and material instances under
  `MCPGameProject/Content/SkyDomeTools/VDB/Preview/`

